-- R23 - Stated p scored as a forecast: calibration block in the weekly review, decision at 40 closed cards, no gate
-- change (both Macro Takeaways digests).
-- Source: thread "Find better approaches from open-source projects", 16 Nov 2026 (open-source comparison).
-- Proposed 16 Nov 2026 with dash.propose_rule; applies Sun 22 Nov 2026 20:00 SGT unless opposed.
-- Text below is byte-identical to the DB row (body md5 aec880899041e9a1695871c31f98bc6c).
-- Data: the 'calibration' block of scripts/weekly_review/cuts.sql; the standing line in build_prompt.py.
select dash.propose_rule($j${"scope": "both", "title": "Stated p scored as a forecast: calibration block in the weekly review, decision at 40 closed cards, no gate change", "body": "P CALIBRATION (thread \"Find better approaches from open-source projects\", 16 Nov 2026). The p on every card is a forecast and is scored as one, the way open forecasting tools score their forecasters (Brier score and calibration by probability band). This rule adds a cut and a decision point; it changes no gate, no fill, no exit and no size.\n\n1. CUT. The weekly review's standing cuts carry a calibration block over the closed linear cards that carry a p: by stated-p band (under 30, 30-39, 40-49, 50-59, 60 and over) the count, average stated p, targets and target rate, wins and win rate, average R; the Brier score of stated p against the target outcome and against the win outcome, each beside the Brier a constant forecast at the sample's own rate would score; and the gap in points between average stated p and the realised target and win rates. The review prints one standing line each week: closed cards with a p out of 40, the gap to the target rate in points, and the two Brier figures.\n\n2. WHAT p IS. p is the card's odds of reaching its target before its stop (the EV formula pays r/r on it), so the target rate is its test and the win rate a lens. A band under 8 cards is a count, not a pattern.\n\n3. DIGEST. Nothing changes in the Takeaways: no card is restated, no p is adjusted on this rule, and the gate arithmetic is as the spec and R09 have it.\n\n4. DECISION. At 40 closed cards with a p, or at the weekly review of Sat 30 Jan 2027, whichever comes first, the review proposes one of: keep as disclosure; a per-band p haircut at the gate in the form of R09 (the stated p replaced by the band's realised target rate, or reduced by the observed gap) for the bands with 8 or more cards; or nothing, because stated p beats the base rate on the Brier score. Until then no card is rejected, haircut or sized on this rule.", "rationale": "Every open forecasting pipeline looked at on 16 Nov 2026 (Metaculus forecasting-tools, the Edgebook and forecasting-tracker journals) scores the stated probability against outcomes with a Brier score and a calibration curve, and the Metaculus benchmark found a calibration correction improves an LLM forecaster's Brier. Our book states a p on every card and the EV gate rests on it, but nothing scored it: the weekly cuts bucket cards by pgate yes or no, not by p. The cut is read-only SQL inside the review; the decision point keeps the house rule that the process changes only when the record shows the need.", "evidence": "Own record at 16 Nov 2026: 30 closed linear cards carry a p, the block runs and its first read is the review of Sat 21 Nov 2026; the numbers stay in the review email and the lessons row. Practice elsewhere: Metaculus/forecasting-tools calibration adjusters and bot aggregation; Metaculus 2026 benchmark synthesis, LLM Brier 0.0999 raw against 0.0934 after Platt scaling on 177 questions.", "revert_if": "Revert if the calibration block fails to run on 2 consecutive reviews, or when the decision of item 4 has been taken and recorded (the cut stays in the review's query either way).", "supersedes": null, "review_due": "2027-01-30", "source": "thread"}$j$::jsonb);
